    "reading": {
        "body": "Nebraska's data center footprint is concentrated almost entirely in the Omaha metropolitan area, with Google and Meta together accounting for more than half of the 21 recorded facilities, both operators clustering multiple buildings within the same counties.\n\nGoogle operates seven facilities split between Sarpy County and Douglas County, several of them within meters of each other by coordinate. Meta operates five facilities, all in Sarpy County, in a similarly tight geographic cluster. That density in a single county is a meaningful signal for power planning: co-located buildings draw from the same substations and transmission corridors, which means local grid capacity, not land, is the effective ceiling on further expansion there.\n\nThe dataset records zero total megawatts. Operator names, locations, and facility counts are present, but power draw is absent for every entry. That gap is significant, because the concentration of hyperscaler facilities from Google and Meta in Sarpy County could represent anywhere from a modest regional load to a grid-defining one. The remaining facilities, including a railroad technology center, an insurance operator, a financial institution, and a defunct or restructured compute provider in Buffalo County, suggest Nebraska also hosts substantial enterprise and corporate data infrastructure outside the hyperscaler cluster, but their power footprints are equally unquantified here.",
